{:tier :glm-5.2, :verse 42, :comments ["<b>Then will I remember My covenant with Jacob</b>. Rabbi Abraham (Ibn Ezra on Leviticus 26:42) said that it drags itself along with what follows it: 'Then will I remember My covenant — the covenant of Jacob.' And likewise 'the prophecy of Oded the prophet' (II Chronicles 15:8), 'your heads, your tribes' (Deuteronomy 29:9). <b>And the land will I remember</b> — its meaning is: I will remember the land, which observed the sabbatical years and was forsaken by them, and I will remember that they accepted their punishment. And it is possible by way of truth that He says: 'Then will I remember Jacob, and Isaac, and Abraham,' who are the sons of the covenant, for all the sefirot are called by that name when they are in covenant, and the land included within them I will remember as part of the whole. And our Rabbis hinted at this, for they said (Leviticus Rabbah 36:5): And why does He mention the merit of the land together with them? Rabbi Shimon ben Lakish said: It is like a lord who had three daughters and one maidservant who raised them; as long as the lord inquired after the welfare of his daughters, he would say: 'Ask after the welfare of her who raised them.'"], :book :leviticus, :voice :ramban, :lang :en, :chapter 26, :notes {0 "The word המדות (literally 'measures') in kabbalistic usage refers to the sefirot; the cryptic phrase 'by way of truth' (על דרך האמת) is rendered literally without expansion per the voice rules."}, :source-witnesses [{"versionTitle" "Sefaria community Hebrew (PD)", "language" "he"}], :timestamp "2026-07-22T04:01:05.034468122Z", :translated-from :he, :model "glm-5.2"}